{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Stranded work RECLAIMED and pushed — Gerrit change 1617\n\nAdopted the unpushed commit ecb71a79f8 from worktree ~/fix-9ec0 (operator-approved above) and\nrebased it onto current origin/main. The DESIGN survived review unchanged — cooperative\n`request_stop()` + a single shared `shutdown_drain_seconds()` deadline, gated on\n`voter.in_flight_reviews()`. Three adaptations were needed, and two of them were real defects\nrather than textual conflicts.\n\n### 1. The stop path violated the cursor low-water-mark invariant (bug 9f63)\n\nThe only TEXTUAL conflict was additive (both sides inserted at the same point in reconcile.py).\nThe SEMANTIC conflict was silent: 9f63 landed while this branch sat idle and made the reconcile\ncursor a LOW-WATER MARK. 9ec0's shutdown `break` skipped remaining candidates WITHOUT recording\nthem in `held_back`, so `_persist_cursor` still advanced the cursor to `newest` and pushed those\ncandidates outside the next pass's inclusive `?t1=` window — unreachable forever.\n\nThat made shutdown the one path that silently drops a gap patchset, which is precisely the defect\n9f63 closed. The original commit's comment (\"the next container's startup pass picks it straight\nback up\") was therefore false on the new base. Fixed by recording every remaining candidate in\n`held_back`. Proven RED against the un-held-back version, using the 9f63 oracle shape: the skipped\ncandidate is NOT the newest event, so unrelated chatter drags `newest` past it — the only shape in\nwhich the defect can be expressed.\n\n### 2. Six tests were silently neutered by a new autouse fixture\n\nMain grew `_safe_review_bot_lifespan_defaults`, which swaps `reconcile_loop` for an idle stub\nunless a test carries `@pytest.mark.real_reconcile_loop`. The six tests here that drive the REAL\nloop predate it, so they HUNG rather than failed — an infinite `while not started: await sleep()`\nwith no cap. Markers added. Worth noting the failure mode: a busy-wait probe turns a neutered\ndependency into a hang, not a red test.\n\n### 3. ASYNC110\n\nRuff now flags the probes' `while ...: await asyncio.sleep(...)` polls. The rule's suggested\n`asyncio.Event` is NOT usable here — the probes are appended from worker threads (the review gate\nand the events-log fetch both run off-loop via `to_thread`) and an asyncio.Event may not be set\nfrom another thread. Waiting on a `threading.Event` would consume an executor thread the code\nunder test needs. Resolved with one `_await_probe` helper that polls inside a single `to_thread`\nhop, which keeps the event loop free — the property the rule actually protects.\n\n### Gates\n\n`make lint` = 0, `make typecheck` = 0, `tests/unit/test_review_bot.py` 90 passed / 13 skipped\n(fastapi absent locally, as in CI's `[dev]` lane — which is why the stop semantics are also\ncovered in the default tier). Broader selection: 2582 passed. The 2 errors in\n`test_reconcile_bridge_canary_class.py` (`No module named 'alert_dedup'`) reproduce on a clean\norigin/main and are unrelated to this change.\n\nPlan-review re-earned in this environment (the prior attestation was `foreign_key`): PASS, 0\nblocking."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786503824920884001,"uuid":"d14252cc-627f-4700-8225-f4bb2cef0928"}